<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="512" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-briefings/">Briefings</a></li><li>Writing an Ohio Appraisal Demand Letter That Holds Up</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Briefing · 7 min read</p><h1>Writing an Ohio Appraisal Demand Letter That Holds Up</h1><p class="lede">A short, precise demand letter starts appraisal on the right foot. Here is what to put in it, what to leave out and how the selection clock works.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><p class="byline">By the Ohio Insurance Appraiser team · <time datetime="2026-08-06">August 6, 2026</time> · 7 min read</p>
<h2>Why the demand letter matters more than its length suggests</h2>
<p>An Ohio appraisal demand letter is usually a single page, but it sets the tone and the timeline for everything that follows. It tells the insurer that you are invoking a specific contract right, it fixes the date that many policies use to start the appraiser selection clock, and it becomes an exhibit if anyone later argues about whether appraisal was properly triggered. A vague or angry letter gives the other side room to stall. A precise one leaves very little to argue about.</p>
<p>Before you draft anything, pull your complete policy — not just the declarations page — and find the appraisal paragraph. It is usually under the “Conditions” or “Section I Conditions” heading. Note the exact words it uses for the appraiser standard and any time limits.</p>

<h2>What to include</h2>
<p>A demand letter does not need legal language. It needs the right facts in a clear order:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Identification.</strong> Your name as it appears on the policy, the property address, policy number, claim number and date of loss.</li>
<li><strong>The disagreement.</strong> One or two sentences stating that you and the insurer disagree on the amount of loss. If it helps, give the two figures — the insurer’s estimate and yours — labelled as estimates.</li>
<li><strong>The invocation.</strong> A sentence saying you are demanding appraisal under the policy’s appraisal provision, with the section number or heading if you have it.</li>
<li><strong>Your appraiser.</strong> The name and contact details of the appraiser you have chosen, or a statement that you will name one within the policy’s time frame.</li>
<li><strong>A request.</strong> Ask the insurer to name its appraiser within the period the policy provides.</li>
</ul>
<p>Sign and date it. Keep a copy of exactly what you sent, including any attachments.</p>

<p>Attachments are optional. Some policyholders include the insurer’s estimate and their own contractor’s estimate so the gap is documented from day one; others prefer to share supporting material directly with the panel later. Either approach works, as long as the letter itself stays short. If several people or entities are named insureds — a married couple, a trust, a landlord and an LLC — make sure the letter is signed or authorized by whoever the policy requires. An Ohio appraisal demand letter signed by the wrong person is an easy target for delay.</p>
<h2>What to leave out</h2>
<p>The appraisal panel values damage. A letter that strays from that invites a reply on other subjects. Most well-run demands avoid:</p>
<ul>
<li>Coverage arguments, such as whether an exclusion should apply.</li>
<li>Accusations about the adjuster or the company’s motives.</li>
<li>Threats of litigation or regulatory complaints.</li>
<li>Long narratives of the storm or the claim history; a timeline can be shared later with the panel.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you genuinely have a coverage dispute alongside the price dispute, that is a reason to talk to an Ohio attorney before sending anything, not a reason to fold it into the letter.</p>

<h2>The 20-day window for naming appraisers</h2>
<p>Many Ohio homeowners and commercial forms say each party will choose a competent and impartial (or independent, or disinterested) appraiser within 20 days after receiving a written demand. Some policies use a different period, and a few say nothing at all, so read yours. Three practical points follow from that language.</p>
<h3>Have your appraiser lined up first</h3>
<p>Naming your appraiser in the demand itself removes any question about whether you met your own deadline. It also shows the insurer you are ready to move.</p>
<h3>Track the insurer’s deadline too</h3>
<p>Put the 20th day on your calendar. If the insurer has not named an appraiser by then, a short, polite follow-up in writing creates a record. Our <a href="/appraisal-workbench/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> lays out these dates alongside your suit-limitation date.</p>
<h3>Check the appraiser standard</h3>
<p>“Impartial” and “independent” are not decoration. An appraiser who will be paid more if the award is higher, or who also expects to do the repair work, may be challenged. Choose someone the insurer cannot easily object to.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Proof of delivery</b>Send the letter by email to your adjuster and by certified mail to the address the policy or claim correspondence lists. Keep the receipts. If timing is ever disputed, you will want more than “I sent it.”</div>

<p>It also helps to think about the umpire before the 20 days run. Many policies give the two appraisers a further period, often about 15 days, to agree on an umpire, with a court in the county of the property — usually the common pleas court in Ohio — available to appoint one if they cannot. An appraiser who already has a few suitable umpire names in mind can keep that step from becoming its own dispute.</p>
<h2>Before you mail it</h2>
<p>Run through a final check. Has the insurer actually issued an estimate or payment, so there is an amount to disagree with? Does the policy require a sworn proof of loss or other step before appraisal? Is your suit-limitation date far enough away, or do you need legal advice now? Have you kept the damaged materials, or photos of them, so both appraisers can see what was there?</p>
<p>If you would rather have the letter prepared, sent and followed through by an appraiser, our <a href="/appraisal-practice/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> service handles that step and the selection timeline. Terms like “amount of loss” and “umpire” are defined in the <a href="/appraisal-lexicon/">appraisal lexicon</a>.</p>
